activation	Compute current body text	2026-10-20T06:59:01.2283825Z text: Unexpected error with Black 25.11.0 when fmt: off and fmt: on are used within block of comments\n\n\n`pip install -U black`\n2. Use the online formatter at (black.vercel.app/redacted), which will use\n   the latest main branch.\n3. Or run _Black_ on your machine:\n   - create a new virtualenv (make sure it's the same Python version);\n   - clone this repository;\n   - run `pip install -e .[d]`;\n   - run `pip install -r test_requirements.txt`\n   - make sure it's sane by running `python -m pytest`; and\n   - run `black` like you did last time.\n-->\n\n**Describe the bug**\n\n\n\n**To Reproduce**\n\n\n\nFor example, take this code:\n\n```python\n[\n    (1, 2),\n    # # fmt: off\n    # (3,\n    #    4),\n    # # fmt: on\n    (5, 6),\n]\n```\n\nAnd run it with these arguments:\n\n```sh\n$ black file.py\n```\n\nThe resulting error is:\n\n> error: cannot format file.py: INTERNAL ERROR: Black 25.11.0 on Python (CPython) 3.11.13 produced different code on the second pass of the formatter.  Please report a bug on (github/redacted)[.]com/psf/black/issues.  This diff might be helpful: /tmp/blk_wtxhgk34.log\n>\n> Oh no! 💥 💔 💥\n> 1 file failed to reformat.\n\nThe error is most likely caused by the inclusion of a set of commented lines that include `# # fmt: off` and `# # fmt: on`. Removing one or both of these lines does not cause the error.\n\n**Expected behavior**\n\n\n\nI would expect no errors and in fact, this does not occur in previous versions. Is this new behaviour expected?\n\n**Environment**\n\n\n\n- Black's version:  25.11.0\n- OS and Python version:  Linux/Ubuntu 24.04.2 LTS/Python 3.11.13
activation	Compute current body text	2026-10-20T06:59:01.2297921Z title: Unexpected error with Black 25.11.0 when fmt: off and fmt: on are used within block of comments
activation	Compute current body text	2026-10-20T06:59:01.2309883Z body: `pip install -U black`\n2. Use the online formatter at (black.vercel.app/redacted), which will use\n   the latest main branch.\n3. Or run _Black_ on your machine:\n   - create a new virtualenv (make sure it's the same Python version);\n   - clone this repository;\n   - run `pip install -e .[d]`;\n   - run `pip install -r test_requirements.txt`\n   - make sure it's sane by running `python -m pytest`; and\n   - run `black` like you did last time.\n-->\n\n**Describe the bug**\n\n\n\n**To Reproduce**\n\n\n\nFor example, take this code:\n\n```python\n[\n    (1, 2),\n    # # fmt: off\n    # (3,\n    #    4),\n    # # fmt: on\n    (5, 6),\n]\n```\n\nAnd run it with these arguments:\n\n```sh\n$ black file.py\n```\n\nThe resulting error is:\n\n> error: cannot format file.py: INTERNAL ERROR: Black 25.11.0 on Python (CPython) 3.11.13 produced different code on the second pass of the formatter.  Please report a bug on (github/redacted)[.]com/psf/black/issues.  This diff might be helpful: /tmp/blk_wtxhgk34.log\n>\n> Oh no! 💥 💔 💥\n> 1 file failed to reformat.\n\nThe error is most likely caused by the inclusion of a set of commented lines that include `# # fmt: off` and `# # fmt: on`. Removing one or both of these lines does not cause the error.\n\n**Expected behavior**\n\n\n\nI would expect no errors and in fact, this does not occur in previous versions. Is this new behaviour expected?\n\n**Environment**\n\n\n\n- Black's version:  25.11.0\n- OS and Python version:  Linux/Ubuntu 24.04.2 LTS/Python 3.11.13
